            <ArticleTitle>Discovery of a Novel Sn(II)-Based Oxide β-SnMoO4 for Daylight-Driven Photocatalysis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1600246</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1002/advs.201600246</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Daylight-driven photocatalysts have attracted much attention in the context of &quot;green&quot; technology. Although various active materials have been reported and their applications are rapidly increasing, many are discovered after enormous experimental efforts. Herein the discovery of a novel oxide photocatalyst, β-SnMoO4, is demonstrated via a rational search of 3483 known and hypothetical compounds with various compositions and structures over the whole range of SnO-MO q/2 (M: Ti, Zr, and Hf (q = 4); V, Nb, and Ta (q = 5); Cr, Mo, and W (q = 6)) pseudobinary systems. Screening using thermodynamic stability, band gap, and band-edge positions by density functional theory calculations identifies β-SnMoO4 as a potential target. Then a low temperature route is used to successfully synthesize the novel crystal, which is confirmed by X-ray powder diffraction and Mössbauer spectroscopy. β-SnMoO4 is active for the photocatalytic decomposition of a methylene blue solution under daylight and its activity is comparable to a known photocatalyst, β-SnWO4.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
